 ],
 "literal": "White mustard seed like small flowers of the tiger-claw tree, / with the fresh, withered flowers of the marutam tree, having spread, / has adorned the water-ghat, his town! Slipped past the joints of the wrist, / so that the shining bangles loosen, having grown thin, / loneliness has adorned the shoulders that embraced him!",
 "translation": "The small flowers of the tiger-claw tree, resembling white mustard seeds, have spread along with the fresh, fallen blossoms of the marutam tree, beautifully decorating the water-ghat of his town. But here, my shoulders that once embraced him have grown so thin that my shining bangles slip past my wrists, and they are now adorned only with loneliness.",
 "modern": "வெண்கடுகு போன்ற சிறிய பூக்களைக் கொண்ட ஞாழல் மரத்தின் மலர்கள், புதிய மருத மரத்தின் உதிர்ந்த மலர்களோடு கலந்து பரவி, அவருடைய ஊரின் நீர்த்துறையை அழகுபடுத்துகின்றன. ஆனால், அவர் தழுவிய என் தோள்களோ, மெலிந்துபோய் விளங்கும் வளையல்கள் மணிக்கட்டைத் தாண்டி நெகிழ்ந்து கழலும்படி வாடி, தனிமையையே அணிகலனாகக் கொண்டுள்ளன.",
 "explanation": "The heroine speaks to herself or her confidante during the hero's absence, contrasting the beauty of his town with her own physical decline. In his fertile Marutam landscape, the river-ghat is beautifully adorned with yellow ñāḻal flowers and fresh, fallen marutam blossoms. In stark contrast, her own shoulders, which once embraced him, are now \"adorned\" only with loneliness (pulampu). Her grief has caused her to waste away so much that her shining bangles (ilaṅku vaḷai) slip past her wrists (iṟai iṟantu). The contrast between the flourishing, decorated town of the hero and the desolate, wasting body of the heroine heightens the emotional pain of separation.",
